Original commit body from 85ec627aa30a97fcf8ba0a8c99daff224b64c2ea:

propagate_taint_ddg read edge["line"] alone. That is the FIRST-encountered of
N call sites for a (caller, callee) pair, so "does the taint reach the sink?"
was silently a question about an arbitrary call. The two guards added with
section 3a -- source_tracked and sink_after_source -- were the visible
symptom: both exist to refuse adjudication when the recorded line cannot be
the relevant site, and one carries a comment naming the caddy case exactly.
meta.call_lines (previous commit) supplies the real site list.

MEASURED ON THE 9-REPO COHORT, BOTH ARMS ON THE SAME BINARY. The label this
moves reaches no CLI surface -- _flow_evidence_dict drops confidence and
analysis_method, and verify_claims hardcodes the literal "approximate"
(INV-karud clause c, still open) -- so a verify-claims diff would report zero
rows changed for a working implementation and for a no-op alike. The label was
therefore read where it is produced, via an instrument wrapping
propagate_taint_ddg, with the B arm stripping meta.call_lines from the edge
dicts to reproduce the pre-change input exactly.

  confirmations 14 -> 16 (caddy 5 -> 6, kserve 0 -> 1)
  flow count IDENTICAL in all nine repos (1428 -> 1428)

The null is the control that matters: section 3a is confirm-only, so any flow
movement would have been a regression wearing an improvement's numbers.

BOTH NEW CONFIRMATIONS READ AGAINST SOURCE, NOT TALLIED (L55), AND BOTH REAL.
caddy cmd/main.go:printEnvironment does `cwd, err := os.Getwd()` at 465 and
`fmt.Printf("os.Getwd=%s\n\n", cwd)` at 469, while the edge carried 454 -- the
first of twelve fmt.Printf calls. kserve bgtest/main.go:handle does
`r.Source = os.Getenv("target")` at 39 and `fmt.Printf("res = %v\n", r)` at
40, while the edge carried 38, a Printf BEFORE the source. These are exactly
the two flows the section 3a guards were written to refuse to judge.

The meta value is validated rather than trusted: meta is an open dict that may
come from an older artifact or a hand edit, and a non-int member would reach
the `sink_line > source_line` comparison as a TypeError.

Found while measuring and filed rather than chased (INV-havos):
TaintFlowFinding.path is not reproducible across processes. Two runs of the
identical binary on kserve gave different paths for 2 of 182 distinct flows --
same source, same sink, same label, different middle hop, both hops genuine
callers. Adjacency is built with sets, so BFS order follows str set iteration
and therefore PYTHONHASHSEED. Flow identities, the confidence histogram and
the verdicts are all stable; this is a reproducibility defect in user-facing
evidence. It cost real time here -- the first A/B read as "30 flows appeared
and disappeared" purely because the comparison keyed on the path.

